// =================================================================================================
// SITE-67 — the hand-authored hub (FVS-G-4). Design: docs/2026-07-26-site-hub-and-operative-knowledge.md
//
// HAND-AUTHORED ON PURPOSE. A hub returned to after every expedition has to be LEARNABLE — you should
// know where the specimen cells are without reading a sign — and procedural generation actively fights
// that. This file is also deliberately NOT part of `config.ron`: it is level data, and the offline
// search must never evolve it, for the same reason `session:`'s win condition is excluded.
//
// COORDINATES. Cells are 1 m (`dungeon::TILE_SIZE`). `origin` places cell (0,0) in world space; walls
// and floor are on integer cells for greybox readability, props and cells are in metres so they need
// not sit on the grid.
//
// LAYOUT — two spine corridors, with every area hanging off one of them:
//
//        z=2   |  [ ASYNC DOOR HALL ]      [  CONTAINMENT WING  ] [MON] |
//        z=12  +========================= SPINE ========================+
//        z=14  |  [ RECORDS ] | [  RESEARCH  ]  [REQ]  \_connector_/    |
//        z=22  |              |               |        [ BRIEFING ]     |
//        z=24  +=================== SOUTH SPINE ========+ [ BRIEFING ]   |
//        z=26  | [QUARTERS] [GALLEY] [ACTIV] [WAR ROOM] | [ BRIEFING ]   |
//        z=34  +---------------------------------------+                 |
//              x=0                                                    x=34
//
// The operatives idle in the briefing room, so reaching the door is a walk THROUGH the Site — past the
// cells holding what you already caught. That is the loop made spatial, and it is why the briefing room
// is the far end rather than beside the door.
//
// THE LIVING HALF (2026-08-02) — quarters, galley, activities, war room, monitoring.
//
// The first six areas each existed because a MECHANIC needed a location (design doc §2.4 names the
// system in every row). These five invert that: the space comes first, because a site that contains
// anomalies is also somewhere people sleep and eat, and a hub with none of that reads as a facility
// diagram. Director's call, stated here because it is the opposite of the rule the first six followed.
//
// Monitoring, the connector and the south spine fit in voids that were ALREADY INSIDE the perimeter —
// the column between records and research, the block south of research, and the strip east of
// containment. The four south ROOMS then run 8 deep, which does extend the building south: floor grows
// 664 -> 932 cells and the bounding box goes from z[2,29] to z[2,33].
//
// That extension is deliberate and it is only in one direction. `docs/2026-08-01-two-live-layers.md`
// made visit duration a priced resource — time at the Site is time the expedition is unsupervised — so
// growing the hub is not free. The south wing is a DESTINATION rather than something on the way to the
// ASYNC door, so its depth is paid only by a player who chooses to go there, and the walk from the
// briefing room to the door is exactly as long as it was.
//
// The WAR ROOM and MONITORING are `RunState::Idle` only. §5 of that same doc records that supervising
// the squad you left unattended was deliberately blocked; these rooms plan the NEXT expedition and
// review the LAST one, and go dark during a live visit.
// =================================================================================================
(
    // Far outside any dungeon extent. The Site's isolation from fog, the knee-wall cutaway, the light
    // field, mould and almond water is achieved by DISTANCE — all of those are dungeon-grid indexed and
    // simply never reach out here. One mechanism, rather than a Visibility toggle on every Site entity
    // at every state change. `layout::tests` pins the clearance.
    origin: (1024.0, 0.0, 1024.0),

